### 2. Introduction

Hey everyone, welcome back to the channel.
I’m a college student just like you, juggling classes, deadlines, and random last-minute submissions.

I used to think AI was only for coding or advanced stuff, but over time I found some free tools that actually help with normal student problems.
Nothing paid, nothing complicated.

In this video, I’ll show you five free AI tools I personally use and how they fit into everyday college life.

---

### 3. Tool 1 – ChatGPT (Free Version)

Let’s start with ChatGPT.

I mostly use it when I don’t understand a topic after a lecture.
Instead of reading ten pages from a textbook, I ask ChatGPT to explain it in simple words.

For example, before a mid-term exam, I used it to break down a confusing concept into short points.
I don’t copy answers from it, but it really helps me understand things faster.

---

### 4. Tool 2 – Canva AI

Next is Canva AI.

As students, we make a lot of presentations, posters, and project visuals.
Canva makes that way easier.

I’ve used it to create clean PowerPoint slides for group projects when we were short on time.
You just add your topic, and it gives you a basic design that you can edit.

It saves time and makes your work look organized without much effort.

---

### 5. Tool 3 – Grammarly (Free Version)

Grammarly is simple but very useful.

I use it mostly for assignments and emails to professors.
Sometimes my content is fine, but my grammar isn’t.

Grammarly helps fix basic mistakes and improves clarity.
The free version is enough for most college work, especially if English isn’t your first language.

---

### 6. Tool 4 – Notion (Free Student Plan)

Notion helped me get organized.

I use it to manage my notes, deadlines, and to-do lists.
Before using it, everything was scattered between notebooks and phone reminders.

Now I keep my subjects, tasks, and even exam dates in one place.
It doesn’t study for you, but it definitely keeps things under control.

---

### 7. Tool 5 – Google Gemini / Microsoft Copilot

For quick research, I use Google Gemini or Copilot.

When I need a summary of a topic or ideas for an assignment, these tools help a lot.
They’re especially useful when starting a project and you don’t know where to begin.

Again, I don’t submit AI-generated content directly.
I use it as a starting point.

---

### 8. Practical Use Cases for Students

Here’s how I actually use these tools together.

ChatGPT for understanding topics.
Canva for presentations.
Grammarly for polishing assignments.
Notion for planning.
Gemini or Copilot for research ideas.

Used properly, these tools save time without crossing any rules.

